        code: { lang: 'text', src: 'SURFACE TENSION\n\nCause: a surface molecule has neighbours on ONE SIDE ONLY\n       -> net inward pull -> surface acts like a\n          stretched elastic membrane.\n\nPressure excess across a curved interface,\nby cutting along a diameter and balancing:\n\n  DROPLET   (1 surface):   dp = 4*sigma / d\n  BUBBLE    (2 surfaces):  dp = 8*sigma / d\n  JET       (cylinder):    dp = 4*sigma / d  (per unit length form)\n\nWORKED: water droplet, d = 0.5 mm, sigma = 0.073 N/m\n\n  dp = 4 * 0.073 / 0.0005 = 584 Pa\n\nSCALING:  dp goes as 1/d.\n  Halve the droplet -> DOUBLE the internal pressure.\n  Hence fine atomisation needs high supply pressure.\n\nSOURCE: TB1 Ch.2, pp.36-38 (8th ed.).' },
        mistake: 'Students apply the droplet formula to a soap bubble. A bubble has an inner and an outer surface, so the tension force is doubled and so is the pressure excess. Counting the surfaces crossed by the cut is what prevents the error.',
