        code: { lang: 'text', src: 'HYDROSTATIC PRESSURE VARIATION\n\nVertical column free body:\n\n  p_lower*A  =  p_upper*A  +  rho*g*A*h\n\n  =>   p = p_0 + rho*g*h        (h measured DOWN)\n\n  pressure head:  h = p / (rho*g)   [m of that fluid]\n\nTWO CONSEQUENCES USED CONSTANTLY:\n\n  1. same level + same connected fluid -> SAME PRESSURE\n  2. pressure does NOT depend on container SHAPE or\n     on the VOLUME above  (the hydrostatic paradox)\n\nLAYERED WORKED EXAMPLE:\n  1.5 m oil (S=0.8) over 2 m water, gauge at base:\n\n  oil   : 0.8*9810*1.5 = 11 772 Pa\n  water : 9810*2.0     = 19 620 Pa\n  ------------------------------------\n  base  :                31 392 Pa = 31.4 kPa gauge\n\n  Straight line WITHIN each layer,\n  slope CHANGES at the interface.\n\nSOURCE: TB1 Ch.3, pp.58-66 (8th ed.).' },
        mistake: 'Students expect a wide vessel to give a higher base pressure than a narrow one holding the same depth. It does not, because the extra weight of a wide vessel is carried by the extra base area, leaving the pressure unchanged.',
        book: { chapter: 3, pages: '58-66' },
        bloom: 'L3',
        co: 'CO2',
