        code: { lang: 'text', src: 'MECHANICAL PRESSURE GAUGES\n\nBOURDON TUBE\n  flattened tube bent into a C, sealed tip\n  pressure inside -> cross-section rounds out\n                  -> tube tends to UNCURL\n                  -> link + quadrant + pinion -> pointer\n\nDIAPHRAGM   thin clamped membrane deflects\nBELLOWS     corrugated chamber extends axially\nTRANSDUCER  same elements + strain gauge / piezoresistor\n\nALL READ GAUGE PRESSURE:\n  atmosphere acts on the OUTSIDE of the element.\n\nACCURACY TRAP - "1 % of FULL SCALE":\n\n  0-1000 kPa gauge, 1 % FS  ->  band = +/- 10 kPa\n\n  reading  80 kPa  ->  error 12.5 % of reading   BAD\n  reading 800 kPa  ->  error  1.25 % of reading  FINE\n\n  RULE: pick the range so the duty sits NEAR MID-SCALE.\n\nSOURCE: TB1 Ch.3, pp.66-69 (8th ed.).' },
        mistake: 'Students read an accuracy class as a percentage of the indicated value. It is a percentage of full scale, so a gauge used near the bottom of its range can be wrong by a large fraction of what it is showing.',
