        definition:
          'A flow is steady if the properties at any fixed point do not change with time, and unsteady otherwise; the test is a partial derivative with respect to time taken at fixed position. A flow is uniform if the velocity does not change from point to point along the direction of flow, and non-uniform otherwise; the test is a spatial derivative taken at fixed time. The two classifications are independent, so all four combinations exist, and confusing them is the most common error in the module. Steadiness also depends on the observer: flow past a ship is unsteady to someone on the shore and steady to someone on the deck.',
        takeaway: 'Steady is about time at a fixed point, uniform is about space at a fixed time, and the two have nothing to do with each other.',
