        code: { lang: 'text', src: 'COMPRESSIBILITY AND DIMENSIONALITY\n\nCOMPRESSIBILITY - a MODELLING CHOICE:\n\n  M = V / a          a = speed of sound (~340 m/s air)\n\n  M < 0.3   -> density change < ~5 %  -> INCOMPRESSIBLE\n  M > 0.3   -> COMPRESSIBLE (Module 5)\n\n  liquids: effectively always incompressible\n\nDIMENSIONALITY - also a CHOICE:\n\n  1-D   V = V(x)        pipe mean velocity\n  2-D   V = V(x,y)      long aerofoil section\n  3-D   V = V(x,y,z)    bluff body in a duct\n\nWORKED: duct air at 60 m/s\n\n  M = 60 / 340 = 0.18   ->  incompressible, fine\n  at 150 m/s: M = 0.44  ->  ~10 % density error, NOT fine\n\nEvery assumption discarded = one term removed.\nEvery assumption must be CHECKED AFTERWARDS.\n\nSOURCE: TB1 Ch.2, pp.24-25 and pp.42-43 (8th ed.).' },
