        code: { lang: 'text', src: 'ROTATIONAL vs IRROTATIONAL\n\nTest is LOCAL SPIN, not curved path.\n\n  rotation about z:\n     omega_z = (1/2) * ( dv/dx - du/dy )\n\n  omega = 0 everywhere  ->  IRROTATIONAL\n\nTWO VORTICES, BOTH CIRCULAR PATHS:\n\n  FORCED vortex  v = omega*r   (velocity RISES with r)\n     solid body rotation -> ROTATIONAL\n\n  FREE vortex    v = C / r     (velocity FALLS with r)\n     -> IRROTATIONAL everywhere except r = 0\n\n  A bathtub vortex is free outside, forced in the core\n  (a Rankine vortex).\n\nWHY IT MATTERS:\n  irrotational  ->  a velocity potential EXISTS\n                ->  one scalar Laplace equation\n                ->  problem becomes solvable\n\nVorticity is BORN AT WALLS (no-slip) and then\ndiffuses outward. Hence: irrotational OUTSIDE the\nboundary layer, rotational INSIDE it.\n\nSOURCE: TB1 Ch.5, pp.190-194 and Ch.6, pp.259-262 (8th ed.).' },
