        code: { lang: 'text', src: 'THE TWO PARTS OF ACCELERATION\n\n  a = du/dt        +   ( u*du/dx + v*du/dy + w*du/dz )\n      ^ LOCAL          ^ CONVECTIVE\n      unsteady only    non-uniform only\n\n  together: the MATERIAL (substantial) derivative Du/Dt\n\n  STEADY   flow -> local term = 0\n  UNIFORM  flow -> convective term = 0\n\nWORKED: steady nozzle, u = 2 + 3x  m/s, at x = 2 m\n\n  local      : 0            (steady)\n  u at x=2   : 2 + 3*2 = 8  m/s\n  du/dx      : 3            1/s\n  convective : 8 * 3 = 24   m/s^2\n\n  => 24 m/s^2 in a flow where NOTHING changes with time.\n\nThe convective term is u*(du/dx): velocity TIMES its\nown gradient. That is NON-LINEAR, and that\nnon-linearity is ultimately why turbulence exists.\n\nSOURCE: TB1 Ch.5, pp.185-190 (8th ed.).' },
        mistake: 'Students state that a steady flow has zero acceleration. Steady means nothing changes at a fixed point, not that nothing changes for a moving particle, and the whole of nozzle and diffuser behaviour depends on the difference.',
