          'A journal bearing consists of a shaft rotating inside a slightly larger bush with the annular clearance filled with oil. If the clearance is small compared with the radius, the oil film may be treated as a Couette flow between plates, so the velocity profile across the film is linear and the shear stress is viscosity multiplied by surface speed divided by clearance. Multiplying that stress by the shaft surface area gives the viscous force, by the radius gives the torque, and by the angular velocity gives the power absorbed. A footstep bearing, in which a vertical shaft rotates on a flat pad, is treated by integration because the surface speed varies with radius across the pad.',
