      'Built from TB1 Chapters 4, 6, 8 and a short passage of Chapter 11, following the syllabus order rather than the book order. The module is the first in which cause appears: Module 2 described what flows can do and this one asks what makes them do it. The momentum equation comes first because it is the direct expression of Newton second law for a control volume and because impacts on vanes make it concrete. Bernoulli follows as the integrated form of the Euler equation, and the measurement devices follow from Bernoulli because each one is the same equation applied to a different piece of hardware. Losses come last, as the correction that makes all of it apply to real pipes. Notches are treated from Chapter 11, where the book calls them weirs, and this is stated wherever they are cited. All page numbers are 8th-edition pages.',
