        definition:
          'The speed of sound is the rate at which a small pressure disturbance propagates through a fluid, and it is obtained by applying continuity and momentum to a control volume moving with the wave. The result is that the square of the speed equals the derivative of pressure with respect to density at constant entropy, which for a perfect gas gives the speed as the square root of gamma multiplied by the gas constant and absolute temperature. The process is taken as isentropic because the disturbance is weak and the temperature gradients within it are gentle. For a liquid the equivalent expression uses the bulk modulus, connecting this result directly to the compressibility discussed in Module 1.',
        takeaway: 'Sound travels at the square root of gamma R T, so it depends on temperature alone and not on pressure.',
        visual: 'sound-wave-control-volume',
        visualSpec:
          'A weak pressure wave travelling to the left through still gas, drawn as a thin front with slightly higher pressure, density and temperature behind it. A second frame shows the same situation viewed from a control volume riding with the wave, in which the gas flows steadily in at the wave speed and out slightly slower, with continuity and momentum ledgers written alongside. The two ledgers combine into the result for the wave speed. A temperature dependence strip beneath shows the speed of sound at four temperatures from minus fifty to plus fifty degrees Celsius, with the pressure deliberately varied along the strip and the speed visibly unaffected. On reveal: the wave travels through the still gas, the viewpoint shifts to the moving control volume and the flow reverses to steady, the two ledgers write and combine into the result, then the temperature strip fills and the pressure variation is shown to have no effect.',
