        definition:
          'The course has proceeded by adding one effect at a time and discarding assumptions as they failed. Module 1 assumed the fluid was at rest, Module 2 allowed motion but described it only kinematically, Module 3 added dynamics while assuming incompressibility and often neglecting friction, Module 4 restored viscosity in a thin layer, and Module 5 abandoned constant density. The engineering skill that results is not the recall of any single relation but the ability to decide which assumptions a given problem permits, since each assumption discarded costs effort and each one wrongly retained costs accuracy.',
        takeaway: 'The subject is a ladder of assumptions, and the engineering judgement lies in knowing which rung a given problem sits on.',
