        code: { lang: 'text', src: 'THE COURSE AS A LADDER OF ASSUMPTIONS\n\n  M1  fluid AT REST\n      -> no shear anywhere -> pressure, forces on gates\n\n  M2  fluid MOVES, but only DESCRIBED\n      -> continuity, stream function, potential,\n         exact laminar solutions\n\n  M3  add DYNAMICS, keep rho constant, often ignore friction\n      -> momentum, Bernoulli, meters, then losses added back\n\n  M4  restore VISCOSITY, but only in a THIN LAYER\n      -> boundary layer, separation, drag, lift,\n         dimensional analysis\n\n  M5  abandon CONSTANT DENSITY\n      -> speed of sound, choking, shocks, CFD\n\nTHE DECISION SEQUENCE FOR A NEW PROBLEM:\n\n  is the fluid moving?           no  -> M1 only\n  are forces/pressures needed?   no  -> M2 suffices\n  is a body immersed in it?      yes -> M4\n  is M > 0.3 anywhere?           yes -> M5\n\nWORKED: pump and pipeline for a chemical plant\n\n  liquid          -> M5 IRRELEVANT\n  moving, forces  -> M3 in full\n  no immersed body-> M4 only via the friction factor\n  vessel statics,\n  cavitation margin-> M1\n\nKnowing which module does NOT apply is worth as much\nas knowing which does.\n\nSOURCE: TB1 Ch.1, p.6 and Ch.2, pp.38-44 (8th ed.).' },
